# Path ownership for the platform lane — the gate that lets a fixer author in this repo without
# being able to edit its own governor. Tier table + reasoning:
# docs/agents/iac-lane.md §"The platform lane — homelab is one repo, so the gate is per PATH".
#
# LIVE once tofu/github is applied (flip written 2026-08-04): homelab runs
#   require_approval = true + require_code_owner_review = true. Every PR needs an approving review
#   (the reviewer bot's counts, and it now sees homelab because the repo joined the platform claim);
#   a PR touching an owned path below additionally needs a HUMAN code owner. Two automated loops
#   Renovate's Actions SHA-pinning (.github/workflows/) gates on you by design — arguably the
#   change you most want eyes on. The deploy-pin lane, devbox-update and the arc-runner auto-bump
#   stay hands-off via the carve-outs at the bottom.
#
# GitHub semantics that shape the layout below: LAST matching pattern wins, and a pattern with NO
# owner REMOVES ownership. So general tiers come first and carve-outs come last.

# ── Tier 3 — governance & supply chain. Never agent-authored (deny row in the tier table).
# agents/ is the launcher that builds its own dispatch command (ADR-094), the scan that decides
# what gets dispatched, and the reflex that approves its own PRs. tofu/github/ is the ruleset
# governing all of it. An agent able to edit these is not gated at all, whatever the ruleset says.
/agents/            @RasmusSoot
/scripts/           @RasmusSoot
/policy/            @RasmusSoot
/.github/           @RasmusSoot

# ── Tier 2 — applied out-of-band (tofu apply, ansible → the router, ArgoCD app-of-apps).
# A human reads the diff before it reaches a machine; the apply itself stays outside the cluster's
# own dependency cone (docs/dependency-upgrades.md §"Where the runner sits").
/tofu/              @RasmusSoot
/ansible/           @RasmusSoot
/opnsense/          @RasmusSoot
/machines/          @RasmusSoot
# argocd/platform/ carries the Applications themselves and syncs with prune: true — an edit here
# DELETES services, which is why it is tier 2 while argocd/resources/ is tier 1.
/argocd/platform/   @RasmusSoot

# ── Tier 3 (continued) — more specific than /tofu/, so they must come after it.
/tofu/github/       @RasmusSoot
/tofu/cloudflare/   @RasmusSoot

# ── Tier 1 — ArgoCD applies on merge, CI is the gate. Unowned by design:
#   SERVICES.md   README.md
#
# docs/ is OWNED (2026-08-04). It applies nothing and breaks nothing, so it looks like the safest
# thing to leave open — but it is the platform's MEMORY: the FU tracker (single-writer by
# contract), the ADRs, the incident postmortems. An agent merging there rewrites the record the
# next session reads as ground truth, and unlike a bad manifest nothing reconciles it back.
# Operator risk model 2026-08-04: workers doing extra or bad WORK is fine; merges to master are
# the only thing that matters. This is a merge an agent could otherwise make alone.
/docs/              @RasmusSoot
#
# EXCEPT, for now, argocd/resources/ — see below.
#
# ⚠ TEMPORARY, and the whole point of it is to be removed (operator, 2026-08-04). Tier 1 is the one
#   path where merge IS deploy to the live cluster and no human is in the loop by design, so it is
#   also the one place "the CODEOWNERS gate will hold" is FALSE. The doctrine's answer for this tier
#   was never review — it is the IAC-G04 policy sentinel, which today runs in SHADOW mode and is
#   pointed at the -iac repos, not homelab. Until the sentinel covers homelab (or agent output here
#   has earned the trust), owning this path makes the gate real while we find out what a fixer
#   actually produces against real alerts. Cost: review latency on a handful of PRs a week.
#   REMOVE THIS LINE when IAC-G04 enforces for homelab — it is a scaffold, not a policy.
/argocd/resources/  @RasmusSoot

# ── Carve-outs, last so they win.
# The deploy-pin lane is mechanical: the homelab-deploy App bumps this file (ADR-084) and
# auto-merges on CI-green. No owner = no code-owner review = the lane keeps working after the flip.
/agents/images.env

# The arc-runner auto-bump commits BOTH of these on every runner-image build, and they are tier-2
# and tier-3 paths — so the flip would have made a mechanical tag bump wait for a human. Operator
# ruling 2026-08-04: keep it hands-off. Ownership is REPLACED, not dropped: `devbox run
# pin-only-lint` (required `ci`) allows only arc-runner image-pin lines in these files via a PR,
# which is stricter than the review it stands in for — a regex cannot approve a smuggled fourth
# line. A real edit goes to master directly, the operator path (CLAUDE.md §"How changes land").
/argocd/platform/arc-runners.yaml
/agents/coordinator/reflexes-argo.yaml

# The agent-coordinator deploy-pin (FU-152). The tag used to be swept across EIGHT manifests under
# agents/coordinator/ — seven of them owned by the /agents/ rule above, so a one-line first-party
# bump could never auto-merge (homelab#113), and the eighth was reflexes-argo.yaml where
# pin-only-lint only permits ARC-RUNNER pins, making the same PR structurally red. The tag now lives
# in this one kustomize file and nothing else, so one carve-out replaces a regex per guarded file.
/agents/coordinator/kustomization.yaml

# The CHART-deploy lane (ADR-084), same ruling as the runner-image lane above and for the same
# reason: `agents/images.env` was carved out so the agent-base half of the deploy lane keeps
# flowing, but the chart half writes `argocd/platform/openrouter-operator.yaml` — a tier-2 path —
# so #104 and #105 both sat BLOCKED on a human for a one-line targetRevision bump with CI green.
# Ownership is REPLACED, not dropped: `devbox run pin-only-lint` (required `ci`) allows only a
# CalVer `targetRevision:` line into this file via a PR. A real edit — prune, source, values —
# takes the operator path straight to master, where this check does not run.
/argocd/platform/openrouter-operator.yaml

# The ownership file owns itself — otherwise the first thing an agent could propose is its own
# ungating (oracle-fleet's CODEOWNERS carries the same line for the same reason).
/CODEOWNERS         @RasmusSoot
